  "era": "post-Civil War fortune display era",
  "summary": "Marble halls, silk walls and imported everything — fortunes rendered as furniture.",
  "description": "Gilded Age interiors converted industrial fortunes into continental display: Fifth Avenue and Newport mansions (Richard Morris Hunt's Breakers) filled with Italian marble, French silk wall panels, Japanese lacquer corners, oak galleries, and rooms purchased — sometimes entire ceilings — from European palaces. Decorators (the Herter Brothers, Ogden Codman with Edith Wharton's The Decoration of Houses) professionalized the field; every surface carried currency: parquet inlays, gilded mouldings, tapestry-lined halls, and the double-parlor social machine. The style's excess seeded both the American Renaissance and its reform backlash — and remains the visual grammar of \"old money\" costume drama.",
  "culturalContext": "Gilded Age interiors professionalized American decoration: Herter Brothers and Hunt's mansions displayed industrial fortune as European culture, the backlash (Wharton's reform writings) shaped modern taste, and the style remains costume drama's \"old money\" visual grammar.",
